.TH MM_14 1 "October 2026" "MM-14 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mm_14 \- RPN calculator with numerical methods, in the HP-15C tradition
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mm_14
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR EXPR ]...
.RB [ \-f
.IR FILE ]...
.br
.B mm_14
.BR \-\-commands " | " \-\-version " | " \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mm_14
is a reverse Polish notation calculator for the terminal. It keeps a
stack of real numbers, 100 storage registers and HP-style statistics
registers, lets the user define new words in a small Forth-like language
with conditionals and loops, and applies numerical methods (integration,
root finding, minimisation, differentiation, sums) to those words.
.PP
With no arguments and a terminal on standard input it runs interactively
with GNU readline editing, history and tab completion. The stack is
redrawn after every line. Otherwise it runs in batch mode: it evaluates
the given expressions and files, or standard input, prints the final stack
one value per line, and exits with status 1 if any error occurred.
.PP
Input is a sequence of whitespace-separated tokens. A token that parses
as a decimal number is pushed; anything else is looked up first among the
built-in commands and then among user words. All input is case sensitive.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-e " EXPR"
Evaluate
.I EXPR
as one line of input. May be repeated; expressions run in order.
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE"
Run the lines of
.IR FILE .
.B \-
means standard input. May be repeated and mixed with
.BR \-e .
.TP
.B \-n
Do not autoload the saved words and registers at startup.
.TP
.B \-\-commands
Print the complete command reference as Markdown and exit. This is how
.B COMMANDS.md
is generated.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH STACK
The bottom four levels are labelled
.BR X ", " Y ", " Z " and " T
as on the HP; deeper levels show their depth.
.BR pick " and " roll
use the same numbering: 0 is X, so
.B "0 pick"
is
.BR dup ,
.B "1 roll"
is
.B swap
and
.B "2 roll"
is
.BR rot .
.PP
Stack pictures in the help read left to right, deepest first:
.B "y x -- y+x"
means Y and X are consumed and the sum is left in X.
.SH ERRORS AND UNDO
Each top-level token is atomic. If it fails, the stack, last x/y,
registers and statistics are restored to their state before that token,
the error is printed on standard error and the rest of the line is
skipped. A user word counts as one token, so a word that fails half-way
leaves no trace.
.PP
.B u
undoes the last change; up to 64 levels are kept. Display settings and
word definitions are not part of undo.
